An Operational Amplifier Comparator(or also called voltage comparator) is an open-looped circuit(meaning that the output does not invoke any action in the circuit) that compares an input voltage to a reference voltage and creates a binary output(0 or 1) that tells which of the 2 voltages are higher. Both of these voltages are analog signals, and the reference voltage signal at the other input leg is fixed.

A comparator by itself is a device or circuit that compares the voltage levels of 2 inputs and then chooses either a high or low voltage, and is a basic analog to digital converter(which i'm starting to see is important since it seems alot of inputs in the car are analog signals but many of the components require digital input signals. An example of application could be a temperature sensor. If you want the heater to turn on at below a specific temperature, you can set the comparator reference voltage at the point where you want it to turn on. Also these op-amp comparators are no-code so its like a primitive like comparator.

Ok so an Op-Amp amplifies the difference between the 2 outputs put simply. If non-inverting input is higher, the output will make a postive voltage that amplifies difference in input voltage. The output is usually connected to one of the inputs to being both inputs back into balance. If it isn't connected, the output saturates to the highest the op-amp can go(in other words, it goes to the positive/negative max boundaries). The same equal thing happens if inverting input is higher. Op-Amps are designed to give a neat linear output. 


